WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.490
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:11.120
We're currently more than 130 episodes into
the series, and we still haven't talked about

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.120
this algorithm.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.820
How could we go on for so long without PatchMatch?

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.990
So, let's do this right now!

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.210
You'll love this one.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:23.390
This technique helps us to make absolutely
crazy modifications to previously existing

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:28.320
photographs and it is one of the landmark
papers for all kinds of photo manipulation

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.970
which is still widely used to this day.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:34.640
Consider the following workflow: we have this
image as an input.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.530
Let's mark the roofline for hole filling,
or image inpainting as the literature refers

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.530
to it.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:44.570
And the hole is now filled with quite sensible
information.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.540
Now we mark some of the pillars to reshape
the object.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.030
And then, we pull the roof upward.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:55.360
The output is a completely redesigned version
of the input photograph.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.270
Wow, absolutely incredible.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:03.230
And the whole thing happens interactively,
almost in real time, but if we consider the

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.750
hardware improvement since this paper was
published, it is safe to say that today it

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.130
runs in real time even on a mediocre computer.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.460
And in this piece of work, the image completion
part works by adding additional hints to the

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.930
algorithm, for instance, marking the expected
shape of an object that we wish to cut out

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.540
and have it filled in with new data.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:29.260
Or, showing the shape of the building that
we wish to edit also helps the technique considerably.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.060
These results are so stunning, I remember
that when I had first seen them, I had to

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.470
recheck over and over again because I could
hardly believe my eyes.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.570
This technique offers not only these high
quality results, but it is considerably quicker

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.170
than its competitors.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:50.490
To accomplish image inpainting, most algorithms
look for regions in the image that are similar

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.220
to the one that is being removed, and borrow
some information from there for the filling

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.220
process.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.950
Here, one of the key ideas that speed up the
process is that when good correspondances

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:06.360
are found, if we are doing another lookup,
we shouldn't restart the patch matching process,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.090
but we should try to search nearby, because
that's where we are most likely to find useful

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.090
information.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.769
You know what the best part is?

00:02:13.769 --> 00:02:19.019
What you see here is just the start: this
technique does not use any of the modern machine

00:02:19.019 --> 00:02:24.019
learning techniques, so in the era of these
incredibly powerful deep neural networks,

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:29.260
I can only imagine the quality of solutions
we'll be able to obtain in the near future.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.639
We are living amazing times indeed.

00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:35.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

